  "apod": {
    "date": "2026-11-19",
    "title": "The Horsehead and Flame Nebulas",
    "explanation": " Explanation:  Today's  image  could have been a painting. But what do these colors mean? The brightest star, left of the center, is blue  Alnitak  (the Girdle):  a triple star system  best known for being the easternmost star in  Orion's Belt . The instantly recognizable shape of a dark horse's head at the center is the aptly named  Horsehead Nebula . It is about five light-years tall and lies approximately 1,500 light-years away. It is made of  dust and gas  that obscure the red glow behind it, which is caused by hydrogen gas being ionized by the bright star  Sigma Orionis  at the top. The orange nebula with dark filaments on the left is the  Flame Nebula . Closer to the Horsehead, the blue  reflection nebula   NGC 2023  is actively forming stars. The data for this \"painting\" were collected for almost six hours at the  Utah   Desert Remote Observatories , but the equipment was  remotely controlled  by the astrophotographer from his home, over 2,000 km away in  Illinois .   Tomorrow's picture:  what's next?",
    "image": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Horsehead_Nebula_Killion.jpg?w=6000&h=4800&fit=clip",
    "link": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-19-the-horsehead-and-flame-nebulas/",
    "credit": " Mark E. Killion ",
    "alt": "Bright stars and colorful nebulas shine on a dark field of stars. A small, dark nebula near the center looks like a horse's head.",
    "media_type": "image"
  },
  "updated": "2026-11-19T09:51:18.948093+00:00"
